  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\n(Monday - Thursday Night)\n\nThe quasi-stationary front is expected to quickly lift north of the \narea on Monday as cyclogenesis commences across the central Plains. \nVery anomalous mid/upper level ridging is forecast to amplify across \nthe mid south early/mid week. In fact, 500-hPa heights of 594+ dm \nwould be record-breaking for this time of year. In addition, 850-hPa \ntemperatures climb back above +20C (>99th percentile). The latest \nEPS Extreme Forecast Index (EFI) is also picking up on this heat, \nshowing EFI values of 0.8-0.9 for high temperatures Tuesday and \nWednesday, with even a slight Shift of Tails (SOT). This air mass \ndoes seem capable of threatening record highs (upper 90s to around \n100 degrees) Tuesday and Wednesday, though it will depend on cloud \ncover and rain chances. Speaking of which, a cold front is likely to \nstall out near or just north of our CWA on Tuesday. This front may \nserve as the focus for showers and thunderstorms each day, with any \nwidespread activity creating debris clouds which ultimately could \nimpact temperatures over much of the area. Therefore, widespread mid \n90s are not a given, though confidence does increase the farther \nsouth you head within the region. \n\nA likely scenario may be for each successive round of convection to \nhelp help augment the boundary and help ooze it southward. This \npoints to a gradual \"cool\" down heading toward late week into next \nweekend. Temperatures each day get more uncertain due to the high \nvariability in the location of the front, and the fact that clouds \nand showers/storms near the boundary may enhance the temperature \ngradient from north to south.",
